% ============================================================================
% reaction-types — the five basic types of chemical reaction and the defining
% token each one is described by (adj-facts-stdlib, CHEMISTRY).
% ============================================================================
% One of the first organizing ideas of introductory chemistry: the endless
% variety of chemical equations sorts into just a handful of BASIC reaction
% types, and each type is named by what happens to the atoms: a combination
% reaction combines two or more substances into one; a decomposition reaction
% breaks a compound down into simpler substances; a single-replacement reaction
% has one element replace another in a compound; a double-replacement reaction
% swaps the ions of two compounds; a combustion reaction burns a substance in
% oxygen. (That summary used to sit in quotation marks; it is a paraphrase,
% not the page's words.) Before you can predict an equation's products you must
% first classify WHICH of these five types it is. Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? reaction_defining(combustion, $Defining)   % reacts_with_oxygen
%
% This is a native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5): each `row` lowers to a ground
% relation `reaction_defining(type, defining)` carrying its row's citation, so
% the lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query — the engine returns the
% defining token WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero model calls, and
% abstains on a word that is not one of these five reaction types (it never
% invents a definition).
%
% Each of the five basic reaction types and the defining token the source
% states for it, as a little truth table:
%
%     reaction type        defining token           a beginner's cue
%     ------------------   ----------------------   -------------------------
%     combination          two_or_more_combine      two+ substances → one new
%     decomposition        breaks_down              one compound → simpler parts
%     single_replacement   one_element_replaces     an element swaps into a compound
%     double_replacement   ions_exchange_places     two compounds swap their ions
%     combustion           reacts_with_oxygen       a substance burns in oxygen
%
% The query also runs BACKWARD — bind a defining token and recall its type:
%
%     ? reaction_defining($Type, breaks_down)   % decomposition
%
% A word that is NOT one of these five basic reaction types — `neutralization`,
% `polymerization`, `rock` — is deliberately NOT a row, so a reaction-type
% recall ABSTAINS on it rather than inventing a definition. That
% honest-abstention property is what makes the table safe to build a reasoner
% on: it answers exactly the type→defining pairs it can ground, and only those.
%
% The `defining` value of every row is a SINGLE lowercase atom, chosen to echo
% the source's own words for that reaction type — never a definition the source
% does not support. (The source says a compound "breaks down", so the atom is
% `breaks_down`, not `breaks_apart`; it says the ions "exchange places", so the
% atom is `ions_exchange_places` — every value stays literally checkable against
% the quoted span.)
%
% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
% Provenance (nothing here is asserted from memory — every type→defining pair
% below was read out of a fetched LibreTexts "Classifying Chemical Reactions"
% page, and any pair whose definition could not be verified there would have
% been dropped). Each pair, with the source it was copied from and the verbatim
% span that states it:
%
%   combination → two_or_more_combine
%     "A combination reaction is a reaction in which two or more substances
%      combine to form a single new substance."
%
%   decomposition → breaks_down
%     "A decomposition reaction is a reaction in which a compound breaks down
%      into two or more simpler substances."
%
%   single_replacement → one_element_replaces
%     "A fourth type of reaction is the single replacement reaction, in which
%      one element replaces a similar element in a compound."
%      (The page writes U+00A0 between "fourth" and "type"; the row carries
%      it. With the plain space shown here the sentence occurs zero times.)
%
%   double_replacement → ions_exchange_places
%     "A double-replacement reaction (also called double-displacement reaction)
%      is a reaction in which the positive and negative ions of two ionic
%      compounds exchange places to form two new compounds."
%
%   combustion → reacts_with_oxygen
%     "A combustion reaction is a reaction in which a substance reacts with
%      oxygen gas, releasing energy in the form of light and heat."
%
% All five spans are on the same page:
%   https://chem.libretexts.org/Courses/Anoka-Ramsey_Community_College/Introduction_to_Chemistry/07:_Chemical_Reactions/7.05:_Classifying_Chemical_Reactions
%   (Chemistry LibreTexts — Anoka-Ramsey Community College, "Introduction to
%    Chemistry", 7.05 Classifying Chemical Reactions.)
%
% EACH ROW CARRIES ITS OWN SPAN (ADJ-TABLES RS-5e, #14986). This paragraph
% used to say an ADJ `table` carries ONE provenance envelope, so the
% combination sentence was the `source` of all five answers while the
% other spans were only listed above. Row blocks now let each row carry the
% span quoted for it above. The envelope is now a framing sentence from the
% page, which names no reaction type:
%
%     "The key to success is to find useful ways to categorize reactions."
%
% Measured 2026-09-15 on the raw page (HTTP 200; a nonsense path on the same
% host returns 404), with inline tags removed without a space and only ASCII
% whitespace collapsed: the five row sentences (the single-replacement one
% with its U+00A0) and the envelope each occur exactly once, each inside a
% `<p>`. Every row's page is the envelope's, so no row restates a `locator`
% line or `trust` (ADJ-TABLES §4). LibreTexts is an openly-licensed college
% teaching resource (not a
% primary U.S. `.gov`/`.edu` authority), so the honest trust tier is
% `consensus`.
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table reaction_defining {
    columns type, defining

    row (combination, two_or_more_combine) {
        source "A combination reaction is a reaction in which two or more substances combine to form a single new substance."
    }
    row (decomposition, breaks_down) {
        source "A decomposition reaction is a reaction in which a compound breaks down into two or more simpler substances."
    }
    row (single_replacement, one_element_replaces) {
        source "A fourth type of reaction is the single replacement reaction, in which one element replaces a similar element in a compound."
    }
    row (double_replacement, ions_exchange_places) {
        source "A double-replacement reaction (also called double-displacement reaction) is a reaction in which the positive and negative ions of two ionic compounds exchange places to form two new compounds."
    }
    row (combustion, reacts_with_oxygen) {
        source "A combustion reaction is a reaction in which a substance reacts with oxygen gas, releasing energy in the form of light and heat."
    }

    % THE ENVELOPE WAS THE COMBINATION ROW'S OWN SENTENCE, the primary source
    % of all five answers. It is now a framing sentence that names no type.
    % Every row overrides it, so it reaches no answer.
    source "The key to success is to find useful ways to categorize reactions."
    locator "https://chem.libretexts.org/Courses/Anoka-Ramsey_Community_College/Introduction_to_Chemistry/07:_Chemical_Reactions/7.05:_Classifying_Chemical_Reactions"
    trust consensus
}
